I think a pertinent question is how long a developer can "intend" to
release a software update without actually releasing it, before the
developer has fallen from usefulness.

Before anyone responds with more automatic praise for the current
devteam by way of justifying any future eventualities, consider:  what
if they didn't release for another year (starting to fall out of the
already lengthy but somehow "acceptable" statistical release range)? 2
years?  5 years?  What's your limit?  At what point in time would we
stop owing them this courtesy and boot them aside?  There is a point,
and for some people, the time has already come.

And before anyone, in response, reflexively tells me again to direct
my own spinoff project, give it my own name, and be a good little
freeware competitor, please reread and consider the sum total of what
I've written in this group this month.  Communal cooperation is
frequently a smarter, more efficient alternative to competition.

